WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.374
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.374 --> 00:00:05.250
I help some fishing fashionistas
get a line on their cold boot

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.440
performance problems.

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.530
Will we catch the big one?

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:08.488
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.488 --> 00:00:10.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:12.570
So last episode, I
helped my friend Carter

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.520
and his new website,
Bait and Stitch,

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.950
which allows fishermen to
meet up with local designers

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:18.599
to create custom apparel.

00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:20.640
We were able to do a survey
of the different ways

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.850
to profile App Engine startup
time and realized a few things.

00:00:23.850 --> 00:00:27.310
Firstly is that GAE standard
startup time is fast.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.550
There's no issues with
the platform there.

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.590
Secondly, tracing
was an amazing tool

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:35.370
to see where your time is going,
to RPC or other system calls.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.310
And thirdly, we could augment
those traces with custom data

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:41.220
to get more info on the
performance of the code.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.460
But we didn't actually track
down what the problem was yet.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.710
So to recap, the
Logging Tool showed us

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.960
that our loading requests,
and a whole set of requests

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.240
afterwards, are taking
significantly longer

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.840
than the average, which is
about 3 to 7 milliseconds.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:58.020
But upon revisiting the
logs, I had a realization.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.480
Check out those time stamps
for those slow requests.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.910
They didn't come in after
the loading request,

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:05.629
but rather at the same time
as the loading request.

00:01:05.629 --> 00:01:07.920
The difference between the
loading request and the ones

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.230
following are roughly
the same millisecond.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.000
Given this fact, the
way we should really

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.791
be looking at this log
data is that there's

00:01:13.791 --> 00:01:15.589
two groups of
requests-- one that

00:01:15.589 --> 00:01:17.880
arrives at the same time as
the loading requests, which

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.040
are slow, and one that
arrives afterwards,

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.660
which are actually fast.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.490
And with this shift in
thinking, I suddenly

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:24.960
realized what was wrong--

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.060
parallel requests.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.600
See, if you have thread safe
enabled in GAE Standard,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.150
then your application can
receive parallel requests,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.830
which will spin off
to separate threads

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.960
to execute work on
the same instance.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.636
This normally is
really important,

00:01:39.636 --> 00:01:41.010
given the fact
that it allows you

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:44.220
to maximize your usage of
the instance that you have.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:46.987
In Carter's case, however,
this is causing a problem,

00:01:46.987 --> 00:01:48.570
since something in
the loading request

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.790
is causing the rest of
the parallel request

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.950
to block until it's completed.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.380
To confirm this, you can look
at the logs in more detail.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.380
You can see that each parallel
request gets subsequently

00:01:58.380 --> 00:01:59.700
slower while it waits.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.920
And in some cases,
it's 2x slower

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.690
than the loading request itself.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.180
But truth is that this
isn't normal behavior

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:07.530
for parallel requests.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.479
After a bit more
digging, the problem

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.220
materialized pretty quickly.

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.530
Carter was messing around
with global variables

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.000
and parallel requests.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.460
Turns out that in the
instance initialization

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:20.367
for Bait and Stitch sat some
code that looked like this.

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:22.200
When the instance booted
for the first time,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.810
it would query NDB to get
some setting data that it

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.370
keeps around for use later.

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:28.920
Now, when you're writing
client-side applications,

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.859
work like this really
isn't a big deal,

00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:32.400
since this type of
thing is typically

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.280
done before your thread
pools are initialized.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.290
But in App Engine,
initialization

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:40.150
can happen in parallel
to your threading work.

00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:41.999
The result is that each
one of these threads

00:02:41.999 --> 00:02:44.040
will be trying to initialize
the global variables

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:45.450
at the same time.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.010
And since they
aren't thread local,

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.840
all the threads
will block and wait

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.130
until the work is finished.

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:53.340
This has a significant
performance impact,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:54.840
since it turns your
parallel system

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.540
into a single linear operation.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:00.580
The solution came from a very
simple realization-- not every

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.480
one of our requests actually
used the global variable.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.940
But since the logic
was occurring globally,

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:08.340
all the threads and requests
were blocking on it.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.050
The fix was pretty
easy at this point--

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.090
change the global
variable to be lazy

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.300
initialized based on the
first instance that uses it.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.830
In this scenario,
Bait and Stitch

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.760
still pays the cost,
eventually, to do the NDB query.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.250
But the trick here is
that it's more performant

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:26.100
when the get CDN function
is not causing contention

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.725
for other parallel requests.

00:03:27.725 --> 00:03:29.100
Once we changed
this code around,

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:32.160
we saw a significant
improvement in performance.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:33.864
Our loading requests
no longer blocked

00:03:33.864 --> 00:03:35.280
the rest of the
parallel requests,

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.860
and didn't create the
same blocking behavior.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.700
Now, obviously this
is a specific case.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.700
But you can see how quickly cold
boot time can cause problems

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:47.040
that impact your bottom line.

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.980
For those folks who've worked in
managed languages in situations

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.280
where startup time matters, most
of this information is old hat.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.810
But in the world
of cloud computing,

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.550
these small
performance anomalies

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.620
have serious
consequences, not only

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:00.150
for user perception
of performance,

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:02.350
but also in pure costs.

00:04:02.350 --> 00:04:04.860
Having 100 loading
requests a day

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:08.640
at 187x slower than
regular requests

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.686
starts to result in significant
overhead in compute hours.

00:04:11.686 --> 00:04:14.310
Thankfully, we were able to peel
back the layers of complexity,

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:15.780
profile some new
parts of the code,

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.474
and save Bait and Stitch
some serious performance

00:04:18.474 --> 00:04:19.140
for their users.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.877
And if that sounds
pretty awesome to you,

00:04:20.877 --> 00:04:23.460
then make sure you check out the
rest of the Cloud Performance

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:24.250
Atlas videos.

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.700
And don't forget-- when
it comes to performance,

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:28.230
every millisecond counts.

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:31.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

